      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term",
      "problem": "The coefficient of ${y}^{6}{z}^{3}$ in the expansion of $(y+3z)^{9}$ is",
      "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u2026+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(y+3z)}^{9},$ we have $a=y,$ $b=3z,$ and $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+3z)}^{9}=(\\frac{9}{0}){y}^{9}(3z)^{0}+(\\frac{9}{1}){y}^{9-1}(3z)^{1}+(\\frac{9}{2}){y}^{9-2}(3z)^{2}+\u22ef+(\\frac{9}{9}){y}^{9-9}(3z)^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${y}^{6}{z}^{3}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{9}{3}){y}^{9-3}(3z)^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{9}{3}){y}^{9-3}(3z)^{3} & =84{y}^{6}\\cdot {3}^{3}{z}^{3} \\\\  & =84{y}^{6}\\cdot 27{z}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\,268{y}^{6}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${y}^{6}{z}^{3}$ is $2\\,268.$",
